1. Nf3 Nf6 2. d4 e6 3. c4 (3. c3) 3... Bb4+ 4. Nbd2 b6 5. a3 Bxd2+ 6. Bxd2 Bb7 { [%cal Ge2e3] } 7. Bg5 d6 8. e3 Nbd7 9. Be2 h6 10. Bh4 { f } O-O 11. b4 Re8 12. O-O e5 13. Rc1 Qe7 { c5! immediately was best here. often times in these structures c5 should be played as quickly as possible. It looks like black is winning a pawn but there is the in between bxf6, deflecting the knight away and allowing rxc5, adding pressure to the backwards pawn on c7 } 14. Qc2 (14. c5 dxc5 15. dxc5 bxc5 (15... g5 { g5 doesn't work as an inbetween move because of c6 } 16. c6 gxh4 17. cxb7 Rab8 18. Nxh4 { black is worse because of weak king and very active pices for white. }) 16. Bxf6 Nxf6 (16... Qxf6) 17. bxc5) 14... exd4 15. Nxd4 Qe4 16. Qxe4 Nxe4 17. Nb5 Rec8?? { Blunder. g5 was best. } (17... Bc6) (17... g5) 18. Bg4 Bc6 19. Nd4 Ba4 (19... Bb5) 20. b5? { Mistake. Nb5 was best. } { Yes I'm winning an exchange but I underestimated the importance of the c5 square. I considered nb5 here which is the best move but didn't think it gave me the best advantage. I should have seen that nb5 gives me the clearest positional advantage atleast winning a pawn. Not being greedy, I should have chose the clear positional advantage up a pawn than a complicated way to win an exchange but grant black piece activity. } (20. Nb5 Ne5 (20... Bxb5 21. cxb5 Nef6 22. Bxf6 gxf6 (22... Nxf6 23. Bxc8 Rxc8 24. e4 Nxe4 25. Rfe1 (25. Rfd1 Kf8 26. h4 Nf6)) 23. Bxd7) 21. Bxc8 Rxc8 22. Nc3 Nxc3 23. Rxc3 f5 (23... f6 24. Bg3 Nd7 25. h4 Ra8) 24. f3 g5 25. Bg3 Nd7) (20. Nf5 Rd8 (20... Re8 21. Bf3 Ndf6 (21... f6))) (20. Bf3 Nd2 (20... f5) (20... Ndf6) 21. Bxa8 Rxa8 (21... Nxf1 22. Bb7 Rb8) 22. Rfe1) 20... Nec5 (20... Ndc5) 21. Bf3 Rab8 22. Nc6 Ne5 23. Be2?? { Blunder. Rc3 was best. } { An awful case of complete blindness. KNIGHTS CAN MOVE BACKWARDS } (23. Rc3 Nxf3+) (23. Nxb8 Nxf3+ 24. gxf3 Rxb8 25. Rc3 Re8 26. e4) 23... Nxc6 24. bxc6 Bxc6 25. f3 Re8 26. Kf2 Re6 27. Rfd1 Rbe8 28. Rc3 g5 29. Bg3 f5 30. Bf1 h5 31. h4 g4 32. Be2 Rf6 33. Rd2 Ne6 34. Bd3 f4 35. exf4 Nxf4?? { Blunder. gxf3 was best. } (35... gxf3 36. gxf3 Nxf4 37. Bc2 Ref8 38. Bd1 Ne6 39. Kg2 R8f7 40. Bf2 Rg6+ 41. Kh2) 36. Bxf4 Rxf4 37. Bg6 Re5 38. Re2?? { Blunder. Kg3 was best. } { I missed my chance to equalize. I did see kg3 briefly but didn't consider it enough. Always apply burger method in classical as well as Checks captures threats. I could have threatened his undefended rook. } (38. Kg3 Rf6 (38... gxf3 39. Kxf4) 39. f4 Rc5 40. f5 Be4 41. Bxh5 Bxf5 42. Rd5 { the game should be a draw with perfect play }) 38... Rxe2+ 39. Kxe2 gxf3+ 40. gxf3 Rxh4 41. f4?! { Inaccuracy. Kf2 was best. } (41. Kf2 Kg7 42. Kg3 Rh1 43. Kg2 Re1 44. Bxh5 Re5 45. Bg4 Rc5 46. Bc8 Kf6) 41... Kg7 42. f5 Re4+ 43. Kf2 Be8 44. Rg3 h4 45. f6+ Kxf6 46. Bxe4 hxg3+ 47. Kxg3 Ke5 48. Bd5 *

