1. Nf3 (1. c4 e5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 Bc5 4. d3) 1... Nf6 2. d4 e6 (2... d5) 3. c4 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Nd2 (7. e4 Bg4) 7... Bg7 8. e4 O-O 9. Be2 a6 10. a4 Nbd7 11. O-O Re8 12. f4 b6 13. Bf3 Bb7 14. Nc4 Qc7?! { Inaccuracy. Qb8 was best. } (14... Qb8 15. Re1 b5 (15... Bf8 16. Ra3 b5 17. axb5) 16. axb5 axb5 17. Rxa8 Bxa8 18. Nxb5 Qxb5 19. Nxd6 Qb4 20. Nxe8) (14... Qe7 15. Nxd6) 15. Qd3?! { Inaccuracy. Re1 was best. } { A mysterious innacuracy. The point is that black can go qb8, preparing the b5 pawn break. Once the queenside opens, black has a pin with ba6, which skewers many of whites pieces and eyes the queen. For that reason it was better to go qc2, which avoids the potential pin. } Rab8 (15... Bc8) (15... Qb8 16. Ra3 b5 17. axb5 axb5 18. Nxb5 Rxa3 19. bxa3 Ba6 20. Nbxd6 Qxd6 21. Nxd6 Bxd3 22. Nxe8) 16. Bd2 Ba8 17. Rae1?! { Inaccuracy. Rfe1 was best. } { best was rfe1. The rook on f1 is doing very little. The rook on a 1 potentially eyed the b9 bishop and also prepares for a queenside pawn break. } (17. Rfe1 Rec8 18. e5 Ne8 19. Bg4 f5 20. exf6 Nexf6 21. Be6+ Kh8 22. f5 b5) 17... b5 18. axb5 axb5 (18... Qd8) 19. Nxb5 (19. Ne3 c4 20. Qe2 Nc5) 19... Qd8?? { Blunder. Rxb5 was best. } (19... Rxb5 20. Nxd6 Rxb2 (20... c4 21. Nxb5 Qb6+ (21... Qc5+ 22. Qd4 Qxb5 23. Bc3 Nh5 (23... Qc5) 24. e5) 22. Qd4 (22. Be3) 22... Qxb5 23. Bc3 Nh5 24. e5) 21. Nxe8 Nxe8 22. e5 Qc8 23. d6 c4 24. Qd4 Rb3 25. Ra1) 20. Nbxd6 (20. Ba5 Nb6 21. Nbxd6 Nfd7 22. Nxb6 Nxb6 23. Nxe8 Bd4+ 24. Kh1 Qxe8 25. Bc3 Nc8 26. Bxd4) 20... Re7 21. Ba5 Qf8 22. e5 Ne8 23. Ne4 Bh6 24. g3 Ng7 25. Bc7 Rb4 26. Bd6 Nf5 27. Bxe7 Qxe7 28. Bg4 Nd4 29. Bxd7 Bg7 30. Bc6 Nxc6 31. dxc6 Bxc6 32. Qd6 Qxd6 (32... Qb7 33. Nf6+ Bxf6 34. exf6 Rxc4 (34... Qd7)) 33. Nexd6 Bd5 (33... h5 34. Rd1 f5 35. Rd2 Bd7) 34. Ne3 Bc6 (34... Be6) 35. Rf2 (35. Ra1 Rxb2 36. Ra6 Bd7) 35... Rb3 (35... f6 36. Nec4 (36. e6)) 36. Rd2 Bf8 37. f5 c4 38. Nexc4 Bf3 *

