1. e4 { [%eval 0.18] } g6 { [%eval 0.55] } 2. d4 { [%eval 0.48] } Bg7 { [%eval 0.48] } 3. Nc3 { [%eval 0.51] } d6 { [%eval 0.52] } 4. Bg5 { [%eval 0.38] } h6 { [%eval 0.67] } 5. Be3 { [%eval 0.66] } Nd7 { [%eval 0.7] } 6. Qd2 { [%eval 0.55] } a6 { [%eval 0.58] } 7. f4 { [%eval 0.22] } e5? { [%eval 1.48] } { Mistake. b5 was best. } { e5 was a mistake. Opening the centre benefits white as white has more space and better developement. I should have played b5 instead } (7... b5 8. Nf3 Bb7 9. a3 e6 10. O-O-O Ngf6 11. e5 Nd5 12. Bd3) 8. Nf3 { [%eval 1.34] } (8. dxe5 dxe5 9. fxe5 (9. Nf3) 9... Nxe5) 8... exf4 { [%eval 1.22] } 9. Bxf4 { [%eval 1.22] } b5 { [%eval 1.3] } 10. a4 { [%eval 0.76] } b4 { [%eval 0.83] } 11. Nd5 { [%eval 0.65] } Ngf6 { [%eval 0.73] } 12. Bc4 { [%eval 0.23] } Nxe4 { [%eval 0.31] } 13. Qe3 { [%eval 0.28] } O-O { [%eval 0.32] } 14. O-O { [%eval 0.32] } (14. Bxh6 Re8 15. O-O c6 16. Nxb4 Ndf6 17. Nxc6 Qb6) 14... Re8 { [%eval 0.39] } 15. Rae1 { [%eval -0.03] } c6 { [%eval 0.0] } 16. Nxb4 { [%eval 0.0] } Qb6?? { [%eval 2.02] } { Blunder. Ndf6 was best. } { this was played rather quickly and without considering Qb3 by white! defending the knight and pressing f7. Don't ever think "oh the follow up is obvious so I'll just play it, especially in complex middlegame positions, you must always verify opponent candidate moves and treat it like it's on a knife's edge. } (16... Ndf6 17. Bxh6 d5 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Bd3 Qb6 20. c3 a5 21. Nc2 Qxb2 22. Ne5) 17. Nd3?? { [%eval -1.37] } { Blunder. Qb3 was best. } (17. Qb3 d5 (17... Rf8) 18. Nxd5 cxd5 19. Bxd5 { the tactic is to win material with a fork at the end of the line. } Qxb3 20. cxb3 Ra7 21. Bxe4 Bb7 22. Bxb7 Rxe1) (17. Bxh6 Ndf6 (17... Qxb4) 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. a5) 17... d5?! { [%eval -0.57] } { Inaccuracy. g5 was best. } { another move played relatively quickly. } (17... g5 18. Bg3 (18. a5 Qa7 19. Bg3 Ndf6 20. Qc1 Bf5 21. Nf2 d5 22. Bd3 Nxg3 23. hxg3) 18... Ndf6 { this variation is much more developed and solid than the immediate d5. Though d5 was a "safer" alternative to a clear small advantage. this line also threatens discoveries on the queen and completes black's developement. . } 19. d5 (19. Qc1 d5)) 18. Bb3 { [%eval -0.88] } g5 { [%eval -0.59] } 19. Be5 { [%eval -0.99] } Nxe5 { [%eval -1.07] } 20. Nfxe5 { [%eval -0.74] } Be6 { [%eval -0.55] } 21. Kh1?! { [%eval -1.63] } { Inaccuracy. a5 was best. } (21. a5 Qxa5 22. Nxc6 Qb5 23. Nde5 Nd6 24. Qd3 Rac8 25. Qxb5 Nxb5 26. c3 f6) 21... f6 { [%eval -1.23] } 22. Nf3 { [%eval -1.15] } Bf7 { [%eval -1.2] } 23. Qc1 { [%eval -1.15] } Bg6 { [%eval -1.27] } 24. g4?! { [%eval -1.94] } { Inaccuracy. Ba2 was best. } (24. Ba2 a5 25. c4 Rad8 26. Re3 Bf8 27. Bb1 Re6 28. cxd5 cxd5 29. Nc5 Bxc5) 24... Kh7 { [%eval -1.8] } 25. Nf2?! { [%eval -2.69] } { Inaccuracy. Ba2 was best. } (25. Ba2 Qb7 26. c4 Qd7 27. cxd5 cxd5 28. Nc5 Qxg4 29. Bxd5 Rac8 30. Qc4 Nxc5) 25... Nxf2+ { [%eval -2.63] } 26. Rxf2 { [%eval -2.56] } Be4 { [%eval -2.65] } 27. Rd1?! { [%eval -4.16] } { Inaccuracy. Qd1 was best. } (27. Qd1 f5 28. Kg1 Bxf3 29. Rxf3 Rxe1+ 30. Qxe1 Qxd4+ 31. Kf1 Qxg4 32. Re3 Qf4+) (27. a5 Qxd4) 27... f5 { [%eval -3.89] } 28. a5 { [%eval -4.06] } Qb4 { [%eval -3.68] } 29. h3?! { [%eval -4.9] } { Inaccuracy. Kg1 was best. } (29. Kg1 Bxf3 30. Rxf3 fxg4 31. Rfd3 Re2 32. R1d2 Rae8 33. Rxe2 Rxe2 34. Kf1 Rxh2) 29... fxg4 { [%eval -5.34] } 30. hxg4 { [%eval -5.12] } Rf8 { [%eval -5.22] } (30... Re7) 31. Kg2 { [%eval -5.2] } Rf4 { [%eval -5.11] } 32. Kg3?! { [%eval -7.42] } { Inaccuracy. Re1 was best. } (32. Re1 Qxd4 33. Qxf4 gxf4 34. Rfe2 Qb4 35. g5 Rf8 36. Kh2 Kh8 37. Nh4 f3) 32... Raf8 { [%eval -7.15] } 33. Rdf1 { [%eval -7.9] } Qd6 { [%eval -8.63] } 34. Nxg5+ { [%eval -8.23] } hxg5 { [%eval -8.34] } 35. Rxf4 { [%eval -8.17] } gxf4+ { [%eval -7.78] } 36. Rxf4? { [%eval #-8] } { Checkmate is now unavoidable. Kf2 was best. } (36. Kf2 Bxd4+ 37. Ke2 f3+ 38. Kd1 Qh2 39. Qd2 Qg2 40. Qxg2 fxg2 41. Rxf8 g1=Q+) 36... Bh6 { [%eval #-7] } { 0-1 Black wins. } (36... Bf